Give the writer a daemon lock and a write lock

A dialog save, a login autostart and a package hook all start the
writer. Several passes therefore rewrote the files of one user at one
moment, and several watch instances ran beside each other.

Two locks of flock(2) in the configuration home bound this. The daemon
lock holds one watch instance. A second instance names the pid of the
holder and stops with the code 0, because one instance is the state that
the caller wants.

The write lock holds one mode body. A one shot mode waits 2 s and then
stops with the code 7. A pass of the watch mode that meets the deadline
starts the debounce again. A held lock therefore delays an edit and does
not drop it. The sheet mode takes no lock, because the four sheet rules
of the build run beside each other and write no file of a user.

The kernel gives a lock of flock(2) up at every death of the holder. The
design therefore holds no stale state and reads no pid file. The lock
path follows XDG_CONFIG_HOME, so each check run and each user gets a
lock of its own.

A session bus that does not answer still parks the pass that reads it.
The locks bound the count of the processes and name the holder, and they
do not bound the bus.

The new check drives the writer over a private home. Every wait in it is
a counted poll, so no arm can stop the build.

The change sits on the branch work/gtk4-theme of the local tree, as the
eighth commit. The fork wyn/qtcurve holds that branch. Merge request !15 on
system/qtcurve holds that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

